g with a  a disease which can be quite difficult  to  accurately clinically diagnose without  that post-morse and post-mortem  histopathology correlation  now one of the key characteristics we're  going to look for in any polygenic risk  or algorithm is how it compares  to a setting risk above and beyond apoe4  you will recall that i mentioned a  couple of minutes ago that apoe4 is a  risk-associated allele in fact it's the  most characterized  of the risk associated to alleles in for  late alter alzheimer's disease  but carrying e4 itself or what may give  you an elevated risk but does not in any  way  determine whether or not an individual  will develop alzheimer's disease  so it's important if you are an e4  carrier and this slide illustrates the  the distribution in a general  uh cognitively normal population in this  in this case from the uk  so about 23 percent of the population  carry one copy of e4 the so-called e3 e4  heterozygotes and these would be  considered to be a relatively high risk  but we know that a lot of those people  will not go on to develop alzheimer's  disease  conversely the majority of people will  not carry e4 but will fit into the e3 e3  homozygotes  group and yet we know something like 40  to 50 percent of alzheimer cases  come from that e3 e3 group so we can see  automatically here that  e4 assessment alone will not be  sufficient  to uh to have any clinical utility in  predicting  the future likelihood of developing  alzheimer's disease and indeed that's  the case  now just before i get on to share some  data a little point here that  um we our job really in cytox has been  to take and build on the academic  excellence of the cardiff  uh research algorithm and and turn it  into  a validated product that can be then  used ultimately in the clinic  so for that we've um we've worked across  a number of different data sets um  including able  in australia university of pennsylvania  emory university and others  and we have had to demonstrate  robustness in all appley genotypes  the algorithms the algorithm i'm  focusing on today comes from cardiff  university  but there are others available too that  do slightly different things  for example the algorithm from  university of birmingham that we've  developed  based on the mtor pathway we can talk  about that on another occasion or people  can ask questions about that should they  wish  the array is a customized array built on  the thermo fisher  axiom array platform for reasons of  quality control essentially and we've  got global access by our partners  piezogenes europeans and other  laboratories  so if we begin to think about the data  now um  why would we want to use how would we  expect to be  able to use this type of approach i  think there are several key reasons i  mean one  as i've touched on already is to  identify the clinical trial participants  at the earlier stages who are most  likely to cognitively  to cosmetically decline during the  follow-up period of a therapeutic trial  so this will allow us of course to  reduce child subjects and the associated  costs  and increase the chances of  demonstrating a therapeutic effect  we said already that we would need to  demonstrate a performance above and  beyond that  associated with apoe genotyping alone  we'll talk about that shortly  and of course what would be ideal would  be able to predict  those at highest risk of meaningful  cognitive decline by which i mean a  measurable  objectively measurable cognitive decline  within the period  of a clinical trial follow-up  and then another point on this slide and  the last point actually is to do with  subtype so the expectation is that some  drugs are more likely to be effective in  some subsets of alzheimer's disease  sufferers than others  and this type of analysis uh  lends itself to be able to effectively  identify those genetic subgroups  and therefore enrich in subsequent  therapeutic studies uh in in a way akin  really more to  cancer drug development and the the use  of  prognostic markers  the work i'm going to describe now has  all been  um analyzed using the the so-called acne  data set at me  stands for the alzheimer's disease  neuroimaging initiative it's a  long-running  uh study that started in 2004 and comes  with very comprehensive  longitudinal follow-up data and the  subjects have been confirmed using pet  imaging so that gives us a high level of  confidence  based on the amyloid burden in these  subjects that the  clinically defined alzheimer cases  really are alzheimer's disease rather  than some other  dementia and that the age match controls  really are controls  and not simply um pre-symptomatic  at-risk amyloid alzheimer's subjects  so um most of the work i'm going to be  describing now has been done  in this cohort there are over 630  amyloid confirmed subjects and these are  very well age matched across controls  and subject groups the mean age of about  78  and what we can do in these analyses is  compare the performance of our algorithm  with other  other forms of algorithm based on appley  alone or  combinations of apoi and other other  parameters as i'll describe  shortly so if we look across the  uh acne data set and we ask  how good are we how could his genes  score predicting  clinical a.d from cognitively normal  controls across  all aboui genotypes in other words this  is everyone thrown in together  we can see that if you do that using the  apple we alone  give an auc an accuracy measure  essentially of about 73 percent  the the apologetic risk score algorithm  without a weak component gives you about  70 percent in an apparently independent  measure and if you combine the two  things together uh we come up with an  auc of 80  so that's a significant improvement  above up a week but of course this is in  subjects who  some of whom will be happily for  carriers  so if we ask the question what happens  if we  look at the e4 high risk subset  carriers within this group so these are  the heterozygote e3 e4 carriers  are we able to differentiate relatively  high and relatively low risk  in this group we can see that the um  by establishing cutoffs using our  polygenic risk or analysis  in this case the top quartile fell at  the 0.61 and above cutoff  we have a positive predictive value of  84  at the auc level so that's that's that's  telling us we're able to pick out  the top quarter the highest risk  subjects amongst the e4 carrier group  and these are the people you might well  want to enrich your uh  your clinical trial with and actually if  you take that  that measure down that cut off down to  give you the top 50  you're still at 71 positive predictive  value  and this would be hugely valuable to  anyone wanting to enrich a study cohort  for those most likely to develop the  disease and therefore  the ones that should be recruited into  your clinical study  if we take the other end of the spectrum  the apple e3 homozygotes  who would be conventionally thought of  as having relatively lower risk  we can see here that we're able to uh to  stratify  in this case by taking the lower  quartile with a negative predictive  value of about 89 almost 90  so we could we definitely want to  eliminate those subjects from a clinical  trial  on the other hand if you took the  highest quartile you'd be up at about 50  percent so positive predictive value of  about  51 in this particular study so you might  want to include some of these in your  clinical trial  so that your indication would extend to  those uh  e3 e3 homozygotes  within the the study parameters when the  inclusion exclusion criteria for your  study  so so if we look at the conclusions from  the work i've just described and from  other studies that we are continuing  to carry out right now we can say that  the  stratification of the e4 carrier  heterozygotes  can identify the subset of e4 carriers  at the highest risk of developing  alzheimer's disease  and of course these are the ones you  would want to stratify and enrich  into your clinical trial to measure  therapeutic effect  at the other end of the risk spectrum  the apoe3 homozygotes  our analysis has clearly shown it's  possible to identify the subset of these  individuals at the lowest end of the  risk spectrum and would also allow you  to identify  the higher risk e3 e3 homozygotes  to include in your study design as well  the the algorithm has the potential to  improve  uh the ability to predict individuals  who will convert therefore from mild  cognitive impairment to  ad well over and above apoe alone  and given the  [Music]  observations that enrichment of the  mci and the other prodromal at-risk  subjects  gives you the highest opportunity to  show therapeutic effect this is a very  important observation  and we're doing more work at this on  this very question at the moment using  the admin cohort and indeed using other  cohorts as well  the algorithm clearly has the potential  to select those patients who are  most likely to decline cognitively  within the time frame of the clinical  trial  and that's obviously crucial because  without that  decline in a reasonable period of time  and therapeutic effects are very  difficult to measure  the um it's worth mentioning that our  genus score and indeed other algorithms  are available for research collaboration  uh at this stage and we're working with  a number of pharma biotech and academic  studies at the present time and  going forward we our intention is to be  to be engaging with the u.s and european  regulatory authorities and look for full  marketing approval for clinical version  of our genoscore algorithm  in the 2021 time frame  i want to finish by acknowledging our  team  and other partners very important  partners  a very talented team here at cytox in  the uk  and we work globally with collaborators  around the world  key partners for us as i've mentioned on  route have been the  dementia research institute team at  